  <p>Relocation assistance is a primary component of UDOT's acquisition strategy, providing just compensation based on fair market value.{% cite "udot-acquisition-guide" %}</p>

  <div class="section-label">Corridor Preservation</div>
  <h2>Advance Acquisition Program</h2>

  <p>UDOT's Corridor Preservation Program has been acquiring properties in western Weber County in anticipation of eventual construction. Under Utah Code 72-5-402, this is explicitly a <strong>voluntary process</strong> — UDOT cannot use eminent domain for corridor preservation on an unfunded project.{% cite "utah-72-5-402" %}</p>

  <p>In some cases, land has been secured through developer dedication agreements. Weber County accepted a <strong>300-foot wide freeway parcel</strong> dedication from the Westbridge Meadows developer as part of its development agreement — 50 feet wider than UDOT's stated 250-foot standard footprint.{% cite "weber-westbridge-dedications" %}</p>

  <div class="callout">
    <p><strong>How to track the State Environmental Study:</strong> Register for study updates at <a href="https://udotinput.utah.gov/sr177extension" rel="noopener noreferrer">udotinput.utah.gov/sr177extension</a>. When UDOT publishes alignment maps in the Draft State Environmental Study (expected Fall 2026), property owners can cross-reference their parcel against the formal public study right-of-way boundary. Public comment is accepted during the 30-day comment period following the Draft State Environmental Study release.</p>
  </div>
